claim:
needs:
title: required means a value, and new can supply one
effort: M
area: cli
created: 2026-09-17T06:16:15Z
updated: 2026-09-17T15:38:36Z

Today required: yes means the key exists. pidly new stubs it empty and check
is satisfied, so the one thing labelled required requires nothing a reader
can use — an empty value and a missing line are indistinguishable to
get_field and to awk.

Make required mean what the word says: the ticket is not valid until the
field has a value. check reports "required field 'effort' is empty"
alongside the existing missing case.

That breaks stubbing, because a stubbed required field would make every
freshly created ticket fail check. So new has to be able to supply values:

  pidly new -f effort=M -f area=cli "Title"

and refuse when a required field has none. -f is also the first way to set a
field at all; 24 generalises it to existing tickets.

Also: new writes every field the schema declares, in schema order, not just
the required ones. Empty slots are a prompt — you see what the project
expects without opening schema.md — and they make diffs between tickets line
up. Order comes from the table, so the table is the one place it is decided.

What this does not buy, deliberately: fixed line numbers. Uniform tickets
make "the body starts at line 5+N" true, and readers still must not depend
on it. Measured 2026-09-17, aarch64 Linux, mawk 1.3.4 20200120, over a
synthetic tracker of 2000 tickets whose info files are five field lines and
a three-line body. Same query — read one field from every ticket:

  one awk pass, line 8 by position          0.43s
  one awk pass, matched by /^priority:/     0.44s
  sed -n '8p;8q' per file, in a shell loop  17.4s

The two one-pass figures swapped places between runs, so the difference
between position and name is below the noise: the field block is under ten
lines and ends at a blank, so there is nothing there to save. The 30x is one
pass instead of a process per ticket, and it is available either way. A scanning reader is right unconditionally; a
positional one is right only while every ticket matches the current schema,
and is silently wrong for any that does not.

The values column is documentation today — nothing checks that effort is one
of S, M, L. Decide whether -f validates against it, or whether that stays a
comment. Validating means the column becomes a grammar and the schema stops
being free-form, which is the deferred "enums with per-value behaviour"
worry in the design.

Done when required fields are enforced by value, new -f supplies them, new
writes the full slate in schema order, and the tests cover a required field
left empty, a required field supplied, an unknown field name, and a value
containing spaces.
